1. Electricity Did Not Have a Model Spec

The essay begins by comparing artificial intelligence with rural electrification.

The comparison supplies emotional warmth before any technical equivalence has been established. Electricity brought light, refrigeration, pumping and communications. AI, therefore, will supposedly become another universal substrate from which prosperity naturally flows. 

But electricity is a physical energy carrier with measurable properties:

V,\ I,\ P,\ f,\ \phi

A device either receives the required voltage, current, frequency and phase or it does not.

Electricity does not:

* interpret the user’s intentions;
* rank permissible requests;
* apply corporate values;
* change its personality after a product update;
* retire one voltage model and migrate the customer to another;
* refuse to power a toaster because the platform hierarchy disagrees;
* insert a sponsored toaster beneath the answer;
* or announce that the toaster has reached its weekly reasoning allowance.

AI is not merely delivered through infrastructure. It is a centrally designed behavioural product whose output depends on hidden weights, training choices, policy rules, model version, prompt, context, tool access and probabilistic generation.

The relevant distinction is:

\text{Electricity}
=
\text{standardised physical supply}

\text{Hosted AI}
=
\text{metered access to a changing corporate decision system}

The analogy establishes that infrastructure can be socially valuable.

It does not establish that OpenAI should govern that infrastructure, that its models represent humanity’s intentions, or that buying access from one supplier distributes power.

Maxwell did not own electromagnetism.

OpenAI is not trying to be Maxwell.

It is trying to operate the generating station, grid, meter, appliance store, behavioural standard, customer account and sponsored recommendations shown while the customer chooses a lightbulb.

