2. Three Mathematical Observations Without a Measurable Variable

Altman previously offered three supposed economic observations:

1. model intelligence grows approximately with the logarithm of training and inference resources;
2. the price of a given level of intelligence falls by roughly tenfold each year;
3. the socioeconomic value of linearly increasing intelligence is “super-exponential.” 

That sounds mathematical because it contains logarithms and exponentials.

It is not yet mathematics.

To state:

I \propto \log R

one must define:

* I: the scalar measure of intelligence;
* R: the resources being aggregated;
* the units of R;
* the model family and operating regime;
* the evaluation distribution;
* and the domain over which the relationship is claimed.

Training compute, data quality, inference compute, architecture, optimisation, tools and test-time search are not naturally interchangeable quantities. Compressing them into one variable called “resources” does not make them dimensionally equivalent.

The third observation is weaker still:

V(I) \text{ is super-exponential}

No unit of intelligence is supplied.

No measure of socioeconomic value is supplied.

No estimator is supplied.

No interval is supplied.

No data series is supplied.

No exponent is fitted.

No competing functional form is tested.

“Super-exponential” is being used as an excitement adjective wearing mathematical spectacles.

That matters because the claim then supports the conclusion that there is no reason for exponentially increasing investment to stop. The premise is not merely an observation about models. It is a financing argument:

\text{undefined super-exponential value}
\Rightarrow
\text{indefinitely increasing capital expenditure}

Conveniently, the company making the inference is the company raising the capital.

