3. The Public-Benefit Fresco Above the IPO Door

OpenAI was founded as a nonprofit, created a for-profit subsidiary in 2019 and completed a 2025 recapitalisation under which the commercial operation became OpenAI Group PBC.

The OpenAI Foundation controls the Group and held 26% of its equity at recapitalisation. Microsoft held approximately 27%, while employees, former employees and other investors held the remaining 47%. All holders now own conventional stock participating proportionally in the Group’s increase in value. 

The PBC structure creates real mission obligations.

It does not turn customers into owners.

It does not give the public votes.

It does not distribute the infrastructure.

It does not give every country a seat on the board.

It does not make the equity collectively held by humanity.

OpenAI’s current Foundation board includes Sam Altman and a small group of appointed directors. Through special governance rights, that Foundation appoints every director of OpenAI Group and can replace those directors. 

The governance structure is therefore:

OpenAI Foundation board
        ↓ appoints
OpenAI Group board
        ↓ governs
OpenAI products, models, infrastructure and policy
        ↓ serves
The rest of humanity

The public appears at the bottom of the diagram.

That is not necessarily improper. A company requires governance.

But the essay repeatedly substitutes access for power.

The users may consume the product.

The investors hold the economic claim.

The Foundation appoints the directors.

OpenAI defines the policies.

The phrase “this transformation should belong to everyone” does not alter any line of that architecture.

An IPO is also an entirely ordinary corporate step. The revealing detail is that the S-1 announcement and the public-benefit manifesto appeared on the same day.

At the front of the building:

BUILT TO BENEFIT EVERYONE

At the rear:

SECURITIES OFFERING SUBJECT TO MARKET CONDITIONS

The prospectus was not hidden beneath the mission.

It was published beside it.

