4. The Feed: A Pigeon Flying Into a Buzz Saw Day After Day

Reading Altman’s public writing chronologically is like watching a pigeon fly into a buzz saw day after day.

A statement takes off.

It survives for approximately one post.

Then the next statement arrives from the opposite direction.

4.1 September 2024: The Remaining Problems Are Details

In The Intelligence Age, Altman argued that deep learning had effectively discovered an algorithm capable of learning any data distribution—or the underlying rules producing it—and that scaling compute and data would continue improving systems. He said it was a mistake to become distracted by particular remaining challenges and expressed confidence that they would be solved. 

Approximating an observed distribution does not establish that a model has identified its underlying causal process.

Formally:

P(X) = P'(X)

does not imply:

\mathcal C = \mathcal C'

Different causal mechanisms can produce observationally similar data. Distribution fitting does not automatically yield causality, grounding, extrapolation or understanding.

But the pigeon had already taken off.

4.2 June 2025: We Have Passed the Event Horizon

In The Gentle Singularity, Altman said the takeoff had begun, humanity was close to digital superintelligence, the least-likely scientific work was behind us and most of the path ahead was illuminated. He forecast systems producing novel insights in 2026 and real-world robots performing tasks in 2027. 

In the same essay, he identified social-media feeds as an example of misaligned AI because they exploit short-term preferences to keep people scrolling against their longer-term interests. 

Excellent observation.

The saw begins spinning.

4.3 September 2025: OpenAI Launches an AI Video Feed

Three months later, Altman announced Sora 2, openly acknowledging that it could become addictive and deteriorate into an RL-optimised slop feed. He then said that if users genuinely wanted to doomscroll and remain angry, the service would help them do that rather than act paternalistically. 

The sequence is beautiful:

June:
A feed exploiting short-term preferences against long-term interests
is the textbook example of misaligned AI.
September:
Here is our new AI video feed.
Also September:
If anger-scrolling is what you request, we shall empower you.

The pigeon has now reached operating speed.

4.4 September 2025: Ten Gigawatts Might Cure Cancer

Altman then speculated that ten gigawatts of compute might cure cancer or provide personalised tutoring to every student, and proposed creating a factory capable of producing one gigawatt of additional AI infrastructure every week. He added that increasing compute was “the literal key” to increasing revenue. 

No causal model connects:

10\ \text{GW}

to:

\text{cancer cured}

The quantity is an electrical input, not a unit of medical discovery.

The argument skips:

* model architecture;
* algorithmic efficiency;
* experimental throughput;
* biological validation;
* manufacturing;
* clinical trials;
* delivery;
* and whether the workload belongs on that hardware at all.

It is the scientific equivalent of saying:

Maybe 400,000 excavators solve archaeology.

It certainly gives archaeology an impressive equipment budget.

4.5 June 2026: Perhaps We Should Slow Down

The new plan says an international organisation should eventually be able to coordinate a slowdown in frontier development when safety and social resilience fall behind. 

OpenAI had already written, six weeks earlier:

“The only responsible way” to meet demand is to build more compute, faster.

It reported exceeding its original 10-gigawatt US infrastructure commitment and said it would expand significantly beyond it. 

So the public timeline becomes:

Past the event horizon.
Most of the path is lit.
Build a gigawatt every week.
Compute is the literal key to revenue.
Only responsible option: build faster.
International institutions should be able to slow us down.

The braking system exists principally as literature.

The accelerator has contractors, financing, land and substations.

