5. “Give Everyone a Personal AGI” Means Give Everyone an Account

The essay’s largest promise is a personal AGI for every person on Earth.

A remotely hosted product does not become personal property merely because it remembers the customer’s name.

Current ChatGPT access is divided among Free, Go, Plus, Pro, Business and Enterprise plans. Different plans receive different models, features and allowances. The pricing page explicitly says limits apply, and current help documentation describes dynamic caps, credit systems, fallbacks and paid overages. 

OpenAI has also retired earlier model generations from ChatGPT and migrated conversations to replacements. 

The practical properties of a hosted “personal AGI” are therefore:

Owned by OpenAI
Operated on OpenAI infrastructure
Governed by OpenAI policy
Available under an OpenAI account
Subject to plan limits
Subject to service availability
Subject to model replacement
Subject to pricing changes
Subject to account enforcement

That may be a useful service.

It is not personal sovereignty.

“As much as they need, where and how they need it” is not an engineering specification. A £200 subscriber can exhaust an allowance. A free user can be routed to a smaller model. A developer can consume the budget. An enterprise can impose user-level limits. 

The honest product statement would be:

Every person may eventually be offered access to an OpenAI-operated intelligence service at a tier, capability and usage level determined by product economics and platform governance.

That is far less messianic.

It is also true.

