7. “Aligned With Human Intent” Actually Means the Platform Chain of Command

There is nothing unreasonable about OpenAI deciding how its own product behaves.

OpenAI built it, operates it, bears legal exposure and has every right to define its specification.

The misleading step is describing that specification as alignment with human intent.

OpenAI’s own Model Spec describes a chain of command under which platform-level instructions outrank developer instructions, which in turn outrank user instructions. User freedom operates inside boundaries established by OpenAI. 

The operational order is:

\text{OpenAI platform}
>
\text{developer}
>
\text{user}

Not:

\text{humanity}
>
\text{OpenAI}

The actual alignment target is therefore:

Behaviour that OpenAI has selected as acceptable after considering its mission, legal position, safety analysis, commercial requirements and product preferences.

Perfectly defensible.

But that is corporate product governance.

It is not humanity expressing one coherent intention through a machine.

Humanity does not possess one intent. OpenAI acknowledges as much whenever it discusses trade-offs. The company then resolves those trade-offs itself and calls the resulting decision “aligned with human intent.”

That is like Ford setting the speed limiter and announcing that the vehicle has been aligned with transportation.

It has been aligned with Ford’s specification.

