10. Public Oversight and the Board That Fired Him

The essay says the future should not be shaped only by the companies building the strongest systems and calls for public and international coordination.

OpenAI’s most famous governance event remains relevant.

In November 2023, OpenAI’s board removed Altman, publicly stating that he had not been consistently candid in communications with it and that the board had lost confidence in his leadership. 

A later WilmerHale review found that the earlier public statement accurately described the board’s decision and rationale and that a breakdown of trust had occurred. It also found that the abbreviated process lacked a full inquiry and that Altman’s conduct did not mandate removal. The reconstituted board restored its confidence in him. 

That record does not resolve itself into “Altman good” or “Altman bad.”

It demonstrates something more useful:

OpenAI’s internal governance failed dramatically while supposedly carrying responsibility for technology intended to benefit all humanity.

The chief executive was removed.

Employees and commercial stakeholders revolted.

The chief executive returned.

Most of the prior directors left.

A new board reviewed the event and retained him.

This is the institution now suggesting international mechanisms for coordinating civilisation’s response to AGI.

The public did not participate in the original dismissal.

The public did not participate in the reinstatement.

The public did not appoint the replacement board.

The public does not vote on the current board.

“Public oversight” remains something OpenAI recommends for the future while private corporate governance controls the present.

