12. The Social Dilemma, Now Inside the Assistant

Altman correctly identified engagement-optimised social feeds as an example of misaligned AI.

OpenAI is now constructing an advertising business inside ChatGPT.

Its system uses the context and intent of the current conversation to select ads. When personalisation is enabled, it may also consider selected signals from a user’s broader ChatGPT experience. OpenAI says individual conversations are not given to advertisers, but the company’s own system uses their meaning to decide which advertisement appears. 

OpenAI has introduced:

* impression-based purchasing;
* cost-per-click bidding;
* conversion APIs;
* pixel-based measurement;
* campaign optimisation;
* and an advertiser platform designed around moments when users are comparing options and deciding what to do. 

That is not the sale of chat transcripts.

It is the commercialisation of conversational intent.

The distinction is:

Advertiser does not receive your conversation.
OpenAI reads the conversation,
infers that you may buy something,
selects an advertiser,
charges for the impression or click,
and measures whether you converted.

The assistant and the salesman now share a screen.

The essay says AI should help people understand decisions and pursue their own goals.

The advertising platform says decision-oriented conversations are commercially valuable moments in which brands can appear.

Those incentives are not automatically identical.

The Social Dilemma does not require a sinister employee manually manipulating an answer. It requires an optimisation system whose revenue rises when commercial actions can be predicted and influenced.

OpenAI says ads do not alter organic answers.

Good.

The ad still appears because the system inferred what the user may do next.

The product has not escaped the engagement economy.

It has moved the engagement economy into a conversation that users may experience as private, intelligent and trusted.

