15. “Built for Everyone” Does Not Resolve Who Supplied the Materials

OpenAI remains involved in major copyright litigation brought by news organisations and other rightsholders over the development and operation of its models.

OpenAI calls the New York Times case baseless and disputes the plaintiffs’ claims. The litigation remains contested; it is not a judicial finding that OpenAI infringed copyright. Discovery has nevertheless required the production of a large anonymised sample of ChatGPT logs. 

The important point for this paper is narrower.

“Built to benefit everyone” does not answer:

* whose work entered the training process;
* which permissions were obtained;
* how value is allocated back to creators;
* what copying is legally permissible;
* or whether generated substitutes damage the markets that funded the source material.

A universal-benefit mission cannot itself establish a licence.

The company’s intended social value and the rightsholders’ legal interests are separate questions.

Electricity did not become public property by copying every book in the village.

