16. The March 2028 Prediction Cannot Yet Be Scored

OpenAI predicts that by March 2028 a “significant fraction” of its research may be conducted by AI systems working alongside researchers.

That forecast currently lacks a denominator.

Does “research” mean:

* lines of code;
* generated hypotheses;
* literature searches;
* experiments designed;
* experiments executed;
* papers drafted;
* discoveries independently validated;
* employee hours;
* token volume;
* or economic value?

Suppose an AI writes 90% of the code while humans select every problem, define every objective, choose every experiment, reject most outputs and interpret every result.

Is that 90% AI research?

Suppose an AI generates 10,000 hypotheses, one of which a human recognises as useful.

Is that one discovery or 10,000 units of automated research?

A forecast needs a scoring rule:

F
=
\frac{\text{AI-attributable validated research output}}
{\text{total validated research output}}

But even that requires an attribution method and a definition of validated output.

Without those, “significant fraction” can survive almost any outcome.

If researchers use AI tools daily, success can be declared.

If AI autonomously produces discoveries, greater success can be declared.

If autonomy fails but productivity rises, the forecast can be reinterpreted.

The calendar date appears precise.

The event does not.

