19. Final Determination

Sam Altman’s essay repeatedly conflates four different things:

1. access to a product;
2. ownership of infrastructure;
3. control of model behaviour;
4. participation in economic upside.

OpenAI proposes to distribute the first.

It retains the second and third.

The fourth is presently concentrated among the Foundation, Microsoft, employees and investors, with a possible public offering providing market access to those able to buy shares.

The company says concentrated power is dangerous while constructing a full-stack intelligence platform spanning models, products, chips, data centres, advertising and enterprise deployment.

It says total automation is undesirable while marketing agents that automate complete workflows.

It says social-media feeds demonstrate misalignment, then launches an AI video feed and offers to assist angry doomscrolling.

It says frontier development may need to slow while arguing that the only responsible response to demand is to build more compute faster.

It says intelligence should be available as much as people need while every current product tier contains economic or technical limits.

It says models should align with human intent while OpenAI’s own specification correctly places OpenAI platform rules above user instructions.

It promises a meaningful share of prosperity while providing no ownership or distribution mechanism.

It compares itself with electricity while simultaneously building the meter, policy layer, advertisement system and vertically integrated generating stack.

The honest version of the plan is far simpler:

OpenAI intends to become one of the world’s largest and most important technology companies by selling increasingly capable intelligence to as many consumers, businesses and governments as possible, while using its Foundation and PBC structure to pursue a public-benefit mission alongside commercial growth.

That is a defensible corporate ambition.

It does not require claiming that the company is distributing power merely because its customers are geographically distributed.

Everyone may eventually receive an AI assistant.

That does not mean everyone governs the assistant.

Everyone may eventually use OpenAI infrastructure.

That does not mean the infrastructure belongs to everyone.

Everyone may contribute data, labour, demand, feedback and revenue to the system.

That does not guarantee everyone receives a meaningful share of its wealth.

The AI future does not become “for everyone” because everyone has an account.

It becomes for everyone only when ownership, governance, interoperability, exit, affordability and economic participation are distributed in measurable terms.

Until then, the arrangement is considerably more familiar:

Everyone gets the product.

Investors get the equity.

OpenAI gets the meter.

